---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { findInsight } from '../../insights';
import { UAE_CT } from '../../consts';

const insight = findInsight('small-business-relief-extended-to-2029')!;
const nf = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-AE');
const cap = `${UAE_CT.currency} ${nf.format(UAE_CT.smallBusinessReliefRevenueCap)}`;
---

<Article
  insight={insight}
  standfirst="Small Business Relief was due to end with tax periods finishing in 2026. It now runs three years longer. The rules for who can use it have not changed — and neither has the cost of using it without thinking."
>
  <p>
    Small Business Relief lets a small UAE business elect to be treated as
    having no taxable income for a tax period, so no Corporate Tax is due for
    it. It was introduced by Ministerial Decision No. 73 of 2023 and was to
    apply to tax periods ending on or before 31 December 2026. For a business
    with a calendar year end, 2026 would have been the last year it could be
    used.
  </p>

  <h2>What changed</h2>

  <p>
    Ministerial Decision No. 131 of 2026, signed on 29 July 2026 and announced
    by the Ministry of Finance on 7 August 2026, amends Article 2 of the 2023
    decision. The revenue threshold applies to tax periods starting on or after
    1 June 2023 and now continues to apply to later tax periods that end on or
    before {UAE_CT.smallBusinessReliefLastPeriodEnd}.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing else moved. The threshold is still {cap} of revenue, the
    exclusions are the same, and the relief is still an election rather than
    something that applies automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can elect</h2>

  <p>A business can elect Small Business Relief for a tax period if all of these are true:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a Resident Person for Corporate Tax purposes.</li>
    <li>The tax period ends on or before {UAE_CT.smallBusinessReliefLastPeriodEnd}.</li>
    <li>
      Revenue did not exceed {cap} in that tax period, <strong>and</strong> did
      not exceed it in any earlier tax period.
    </li>
    <li>It is not a Qualifying Free Zone Person.</li>
    <li>
      It is not a member of a multinational group with consolidated revenue of
      AED 3.15 billion or more.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The test that catches people out</h2>

  <p>
    The revenue test looks backwards. One tax period above {cap} ends the
    relief for every period after it, even if revenue falls back below the
    threshold later. A business that had one strong year is out for good.
  </p>

  <section class="nt-table-scroll" aria-label="Example: when a business can elect" tabindex="0">
    <table class="nt-table">
      <caption>A business with a calendar year end. Revenue in {UAE_CT.currency}.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Tax period</th>
          <th scope="col" class="nt-fig">Revenue</th>
          <th scope="col">Can it elect?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">2024</th>
          <td class="nt-fig">2,400,000</td>
          <td>Yes — within the threshold</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">2025</th>
          <td class="nt-fig">3,200,000</td>
          <td>No — above the threshold</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">2026</th>
          <td class="nt-fig">2,800,000</td>
          <td>No — 2025 was above it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">2027 to 2029</th>
          <td class="nt-fig">under 3,000,000</td>
          <td>No — 2025 was above it</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <p>
    The extension does not reopen the door for anyone who has already passed
    through it. It only matters to businesses that have stayed under the
    threshold in every period so far.
  </p>

  <h2>What electing costs</h2>

  <p>
    The relief removes the tax for the period. It also removes two things
    that would otherwise carry forward:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      A <strong>tax loss</strong> incurred in a period for which the relief is
      elected cannot be carried forward to a later period.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Net interest expenditure</strong> disallowed in that period
      cannot be carried forward either.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Losses from earlier periods in which the relief was not elected are not
    affected. But a business that expects a loss this year should think
    before electing. With no taxable income there is no tax to save, and
    electing gives up a loss that could reduce tax in a later year — when the
    business has grown past the threshold and can no longer elect at all.
  </p>

  <h2>What does not change</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      The business must still register for Corporate Tax and file a return for
      every period. The election is made in the return, period by period.
    </li>
    <li>
      The records still have to support the revenue figure. Revenue is the
      gross amount in the financial statements, not profit.
    </li>
    <li>
      The Federal Tax Authority can deny the relief where a business has been
      artificially separated — split into several entities — to keep each
      under the threshold.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check your own position</h2>

  <p>
    Our free <a href="/platform/small-business-relief-check/">Small Business
    Relief Check</a> asks six questions and tells you whether the relief is
    available for the period, and what electing would cost you. It runs in
    your browser and sends nothing anywhere. If you would rather see the tax
    without the relief, the <a href="/platform/corporate-tax/#estimator">Corporate
    Tax estimator</a> does that.
  </p>

